      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- POST /files -> FileMetadata\n  body: FileMetaData  (unknow fields canbe null or can entirely a diff entity)",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- POST /files -> FileMetadata\n  body: FileMetaData  (unknow fields canbe null or can entirely a diff entity)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7kwj2L64Ec13pCdx_As8r",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1876.6700005238224,
      "y": 3102.3767692252504,
      "width": 1465.9189453125,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 334353768,
      "version": 1827,
      "versionNonce": 909657112,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1761394599344,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Upload Path:\n- Client uploads file which sends a POST req with filemetadata as body to fileservice.\n- Fileservice requests a presigned url from S3 (which is available for a certain duration & other configs)\n- Creates/Updates filemetadata obj received as part of req body with url & other fields \n- Stores this in DB & sends it back to client as response.\n- Client then uses this URL to directly upload file to S3.\n- Once upload is complete, client sends another request to fileservice to update the status of filemetadata in DB\n    - Good practise is to \"Trust but verify\"\n    - hence file service checks the S3 if the file has actually completed the upload & only then it will update the DB.\n    - Ow if client is malicious, he can send false req & this can make S3 & DB out of sync, which can be a headache for engineers to debug\n    - Instead of client sending a req to update status, another way to do this is to setup CDC i.e S3 Notifications.\n        - S3 notifies fileservices whenever change to file, and fileservice updates the status in DB\n        - This adds slight complexity to setup & S3 doesnt have CDC at chunk level, but only at file level, so lets stick to \"Trust but verify\" approch ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Upload Path:\n- Client uploads file which sends a POST req with filemetadata as body to fileservice.\n- Fileservice requests a presigned url from S3 (which is available for a certain duration & other configs)\n- Creates/Updates filemetadata obj received as part of req body with url & other fields \n- Stores this in DB & sends it back to client as response.\n- Client then uses this URL to directly upload file to S3.\n- Once upload is complete, client sends another request to fileservice to update the status of filemetadata in DB\n    - Good practise is to \"Trust but verify\"\n    - hence file service checks the S3 if the file has actually completed the upload & only then it will update the DB.\n    - Ow if client is malicious, he can send false req & this can make S3 & DB out of sync, which can be a headache for engineers to debug\n    - Instead of client sending a req to update status, another way to do this is to setup CDC i.e S3 Notifications.\n        - S3 notifies fileservices whenever change to file, and fileservice updates the status in DB\n        - This adds slight complexity to setup & S3 doesnt have CDC at chunk level, but only at file level, so lets stick to \"Trust but verify\" approch ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "eg3LNPk590WiF_qLocPck",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -120.26835463170096,
      "y": 3984.9535092400024,
      "width": 2577.174072265625,
      "height": 1120,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3u",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1106045288,
      "version": 4318,
      "versionNonce": 1919399784,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1761485230203,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Resumable Uploads & Downloads:\n    - If 50GB file & 100MB/s = t- 100mb  -  1s\n        - 50*10^3 mb - ? = 500s = 1hr 12min\n    - Chunking:\n        - Divide file into 5mb chunks & upload them (can upload chunks parallely using multithreading on clients)\n        - Fingerprinting:\n            - use the hash of each chunk as the id for the chunk in DB.\n            - UUID shouldnt be used as we want the same id for a chunk incase of reupload due to failure.\n        - S3 presigned-multipart upload feature to stitch the parts together into single object. (Read more abt it on onenote)\n    - Resumable Upload Path:\n        - same as above , but now instead of file, we send the chunks.\n        - When client manually uploads a file:\n            - file gets chunked, do fingerprinting, & prepare the filemetadata with chunks array & status of each chunk as 'UPLOAD_STARTED'\n            - we get the S3 links for each chunk as response and we start sending chunks to S3, once success, send req to fileservice to update the status \n                of the chunk.\n        - Incase an upload fails in between & upload has to be restarted, the app sends the same upload req & as response it gets the chunk\n            info from which it gets to know what all chunks were uploaded & doesnt upload them again.\n        - Once all parts are uploaded, we send manifest file as well. Full file doesnt need to be constructed on S3.\n    - Resumable Downloads:\n        - Steps Client App should do for parallel & resumable downloads:\n            1. Get fileMetaData which has the s3links to chuncks & manifest.\n            2. Pull the chunks parallely & reassemble them to a single file.\n            3. Incase of failure during download & restart, local state can be compared and pull only missing chunks.\n    - Above upload & download works for both client apps as well as web apps (i.e desktop apps as well as browser apps(within js)) - dont mention this unless asked, this can result it lots \n        of followups since dropbox doesnt actually support resumable uploads & downloads from web apps.\n    - Extra Usecase - Direct Download (using curl,wget etc) - dont mention this as well.\n        - for this , the client like curl are not capable of doing reassembly etc, hence the servers (have block servers) has to reassemble and send the full file.\n        - this wont be resumable or parallel.\n        - or else there should be a extra job which reassembles and stores the full obj in s3 directly.\n    \n\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Resumable Uploads & Downloads:\n    - If 50GB file & 100MB/s = t- 100mb  -  1s\n        - 50*10^3 mb - ? = 500s = 1hr 12min\n    - Chunking:\n        - Divide file into 5mb chunks & upload them (can upload chunks parallely using multithreading on clients)\n        - Fingerprinting:\n            - use the hash of each chunk as the id for the chunk in DB.\n            - UUID shouldnt be used as we want the same id for a chunk incase of reupload due to failure.\n        - S3 presigned-multipart upload feature to stitch the parts together into single object. (Read more abt it on onenote)\n    - Resumable Upload Path:\n        - same as above , but now instead of file, we send the chunks.\n        - When client manually uploads a file:\n            - file gets chunked, do fingerprinting, & prepare the filemetadata with chunks array & status of each chunk as 'UPLOAD_STARTED'\n            - we get the S3 links for each chunk as response and we start sending chunks to S3, once success, send req to fileservice to update the status \n                of the chunk.\n        - Incase an upload fails in between & upload has to be restarted, the app sends the same upload req & as response it gets the chunk\n            info from which it gets to know what all chunks were uploaded & doesnt upload them again.\n        - Once all parts are uploaded, we send manifest file as well. Full file doesnt need to be constructed on S3.\n    - Resumable Downloads:\n        - Steps Client App should do for parallel & resumable downloads:\n            1. Get fileMetaData which has the s3links to chuncks & manifest.\n            2. Pull the chunks parallely & reassemble them to a single file.\n            3. Incase of failure during download & restart, local state can be compared and pull only missing chunks.\n    - Above upload & download works for both client apps as well as web apps (i.e desktop apps as well as browser apps(within js)) - dont mention this unless asked, this can result it lots \n        of followups since dropbox doesnt actually support resumable uploads & downloads from web apps.\n    - Extra Usecase - Direct Download (using curl,wget etc) - dont mention this as well.\n        - for this , the client like curl are not capable of doing reassembly etc, hence the servers (have block servers) has to reassemble and send the full file.\n        - this wont be resumable or parallel.\n        - or else there should be a extra job which reassembles and stores the full obj in s3 directly.\n    \n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "EzmO4W528TDw1wux11o7P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1570.8727492450475,
      "y": 3561.160525335057,
      "width": 130.3199462890625,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
