      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762614083144,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Latency:\n    - TimeLine load:\n        - SQL:\n            SELECT t.id, t.content, t.urls, t.timestamp\n            FROM Followers as f\n            INNER JOIN Tweets as t ON f.userId = t.postedBy\n            WHERE f.followerId = :currUser AND t.timestamp > :pageCursor\n            ORDER BY t.timestamp DESC\n            LIMIT :limit \n\n        - Full Table scan without index:\n            - Tweet table \n                rows = 100M DAU * 1 /day * (365 days - assuming app operating since an year)\n                    = 40B rows\n                data = (single tweet = 250 char * 2B/char = 500b ~= 1KB ) * 40B\n                     = 40TB\n            - Followers Table\n                rows = 100M * each user can follow 1k \n                     = 100B\n                data = 100GB\n            - Time to full scan:\n                 ssd read throughput = 1GB/s\n                 time to full scan = (40TB+100GB)/1GB ~= 40Ks ~= 1/2 day \n                                    (why 40TB+100GB & not 40TB*100GB ? -> becoz database does optimizations by creating inmemory structures for joins like maps etc) \n                                    ( sql query time != Disk I/O time, but Disk I/O is a major contributor for sql query time, hence using this for calculations)\n        \n        - With index on (f.followerId,f.userId) & (t.postedBy,t.timestamp) :\n            rows = 1k follower rows * (1 tweet/day => 365) = 365k tweet rows\n            data = 365k * 1KB = 365MB = 0.4GB\n            time ~= 400ms (better but not the best. Also this latency keeps increasing as years pass by)\n                    (we want this to be in single digit ms, as network transport will be 200ms)\n        - Approaches:\n            - limit feed only to last month ~= 40ms (but not a good approach. we should be limiting on total tweets, not tweets/user)\n                - SQL:\n                    SELECT t.id, t.content, t.urls, t.timestamp\n                    FROM Followers as f\n                    INNER JOIN Tweets as t ON f.userId = t.postedBy\n                    WHERE f.followerId = :currUser AND t.timestamp >= NOW() - INTERVAL '1 month'\n                    ORDER BY t.timestamp DESC;\n            - Precompute feed for every user & cap the size of feed maybe to 500 tweets in total:\n                - When a user creates a tweet, update this tweet into all follower's feeds async\n                    - 500 rows = 500KM ~= 1MB  => super fast query\n                \n\n                \n                     ",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Latency:\n    - TimeLine load:\n        - SQL:\n            SELECT t.id, t.content, t.urls, t.timestamp\n            FROM Followers as f\n            INNER JOIN Tweets as t ON f.userId = t.postedBy\n            WHERE f.followerId = :currUser AND t.timestamp > :pageCursor\n            ORDER BY t.timestamp DESC\n            LIMIT :limit \n\n        - Full Table scan without index:\n            - Tweet table \n                rows = 100M DAU * 1 /day * (365 days - assuming app operating since an year)\n                    = 40B rows\n                data = (single tweet = 250 char * 2B/char = 500b ~= 1KB ) * 40B\n                     = 40TB\n            - Followers Table\n                rows = 100M * each user can follow 1k \n                     = 100B\n                data = 100GB\n            - Time to full scan:\n                 ssd read throughput = 1GB/s\n                 time to full scan = (40TB+100GB)/1GB ~= 40Ks ~= 1/2 day \n                                    (why 40TB+100GB & not 40TB*100GB ? -> becoz database does optimizations by creating inmemory structures for joins like maps etc) \n                                    ( sql query time != Disk I/O time, but Disk I/O is a major contributor for sql query time, hence using this for calculations)\n        \n        - With index on (f.followerId,f.userId) & (t.postedBy,t.timestamp) :\n            rows = 1k follower rows * (1 tweet/day => 365) = 365k tweet rows\n            data = 365k * 1KB = 365MB = 0.4GB\n            time ~= 400ms (better but not the best. Also this latency keeps increasing as years pass by)\n                    (we want this to be in single digit ms, as network transport will be 200ms)\n        - Approaches:\n            - limit feed only to last month ~= 40ms (but not a good approach. we should be limiting on total tweets, not tweets/user)\n                - SQL:\n                    SELECT t.id, t.content, t.urls, t.timestamp\n                    FROM Followers as f\n                    INNER JOIN Tweets as t ON f.userId = t.postedBy\n                    WHERE f.followerId = :currUser AND t.timestamp >= NOW() - INTERVAL '1 month'\n                    ORDER BY t.timestamp DESC;\n            - Precompute feed for every user & cap the size of feed maybe to 500 tweets in total:\n                - When a user creates a tweet, update this tweet into all follower's feeds async\n                    - 500 rows = 500KM ~= 1MB  => super fast query\n                \n\n                \n                     ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
