    {
      "id": "CtNcwRtDnBg_opo3iIIjg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20319.27619900971,
      "y": 625.3087681712983,
      "width": 404.7994384765625,
      "height": 420,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 621672044,
      "version": 416,
      "versionNonce": 431428930,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762756163403,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Top-K all time:\n\nUPDATE VideoViewsHourly\nSET views = views + 1\nWHERE videoId = :videoId;\n\nSELECT *\nFROM VideoViewsAllTime\nORDER BY views DESC\nLIMIT :k \n\n\n=> Reading all 1B rows & sorting them\n    - 10Bytes each row => 10GB data\n    => with 1GB/s , it takes 10s just to load\n        the data into memory\n    => Even for update, 1B rows has to be read\n\n=> Use index on views\n    - 1k rows => 10KB data => super fast\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Top-K all time:\n\nUPDATE VideoViewsHourly\nSET views = views + 1\nWHERE videoId = :videoId;\n\nSELECT *\nFROM VideoViewsAllTime\nORDER BY views DESC\nLIMIT :k \n\n\n=> Reading all 1B rows & sorting them\n    - 10Bytes each row => 10GB data\n    => with 1GB/s , it takes 10s just to load\n        the data into memory\n    => Even for update, 1B rows has to be read\n\n=> Use index on views\n    - 1k rows => 10KB data => super fast\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DwfSEMlHKEvuEUVmQBJnf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20904.597888735527,
      "y": 617.7588620547067,
      "width": 703.9990234375,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9k",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 937018270,
      "version": 464,
      "versionNonce": 1299163806,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762755009117,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Top-K last hour:\n\n- when a view comes, find the flooredHour from timestamp & increment views only \n    for the row that matches the flooredHourTime. If it doesnt exist, create it.\n\nUPDATE VideoViewsHourly\nSET views = views + 1\nWHERE flooredHourTime = :flooredHourTime AND videoId = :videoId;\n\n\nSELECT *\nFROM VideoViewsHourly\nWHERE flooredHourTime = date_trunc('hour,now() - INTERVAL '1 hour')\nORDER BY views DESC\nLIMIT :k ",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Top-K last hour:\n\n- when a view comes, find the flooredHour from timestamp & increment views only \n    for the row that matches the flooredHourTime. If it doesnt exist, create it.\n\nUPDATE VideoViewsHourly\nSET views = views + 1\nWHERE flooredHourTime = :flooredHourTime AND videoId = :videoId;\n\n\nSELECT *\nFROM VideoViewsHourly\nWHERE flooredHourTime = date_trunc('hour,now() - INTERVAL '1 hour')\nORDER BY views DESC\nLIMIT :k ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "G_nRY1u2per4dDcW961Aj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20911.717361149764,
      "y": 1009.8774965618036,
      "width": 351.99951171875,
      "height": 20,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 649245214,
      "version": 86,
      "versionNonce": 1002939038,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762754957006,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "=> Use index on (flooredHourTime, views)",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "=> Use index on (flooredHourTime, views)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "-383WlWIAy2NrkJh_XjMP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 20316.054835912426,
      "y": 1101.335334498515,
      "width": 1117.598388671875,
      "height": 960,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9n",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1176282882,
      "version": 3221,
      "versionNonce": 1378024094,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1762762473523,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Throughput:\n- 100k views/s\n    Writes:\n        - 100k * (4 - alltime,houraly,weekly,monthly) = 400k\n        - each postgres TPS = 10k/s, \n            but here due to high lock contention, it can come down to (100-1k)/sec \n        - sharding:\n            - shard by videoId: \n                - For non-celeb videos, TPS = 10k/s\n                - Shards with celeb videos, TPS = (100-1k)/s\n                    - Would be in 100s of shards\n                    - Also sometimes 1shard/video for celeb is also not enough.\n            - shard by (videoId+randomness):\n                 - Celeb videos distributed accross shards equally (better) \n                    => lock contention reduced by (N, no. of shards), but still exists\n                    => < 100 shards \n                    => still very costly\n                 - Queries latency will slightly increase as has to merge the results from all shards.\n\n        - Main problem with above is, handling locking on disk. \n            - If we can handle the lock contention in memory, that would be a better solution than sharding\n            - Options:\n                - Kafka Streams: Let the view consumer aggregate the results with windowing(can be tumbling or sliding window),\n                  and only push the results to db only every once a sec.\n                    - Throughput of kafka = 100k/s per partition\n                        - But due to aggregations & lock-contentions , this can also come down by a huge number\n                        - partition by videoId:\n                            - Not sure if a single partition can handle a celeb, but better than a single shard.\n                        - partition by (videoId+randomness):\n                            - Best\n                            - Here also kafka has to be horizontally scaled, but to very less number compared to shards.\n                - KStreams VS sharding:\n                    - Adv:\n                        - Less cost:\n                            - No sharding, single instance of postgres. Scaling kafka wont be very costly.\n                            - Both has to be partionioned/sharded by (videoId+randomness), \n                                but the no of kafka consumers needed << no of shards needed.\n                            - This due to:\n                                - In-memory lock contention handling\n                                - Kafka has high throughput than postgres\n                        - Queries faster as No extra merging layer needed, as single postgres instance.\n                    - DisAdv:\n                        - Data loss for unflushed aggregates, as only one consumer consumes from a particular partition.\n                - Strategies to address data loss from consumer:\n                    - Kafka has the data, so reconsume from where data is lost.\n                    - Can use checkpointing to avoid consuming more data during failure.\n                        - every few ms, store the aggregates to s3 \n                    - Flink does this all this by default. So use Flink over KStreams. ",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Throughput:\n- 100k views/s\n    Writes:\n        - 100k * (4 - alltime,houraly,weekly,monthly) = 400k\n        - each postgres TPS = 10k/s, \n            but here due to high lock contention, it can come down to (100-1k)/sec \n        - sharding:\n            - shard by videoId: \n                - For non-celeb videos, TPS = 10k/s\n                - Shards with celeb videos, TPS = (100-1k)/s\n                    - Would be in 100s of shards\n                    - Also sometimes 1shard/video for celeb is also not enough.\n            - shard by (videoId+randomness):\n                 - Celeb videos distributed accross shards equally (better) \n                    => lock contention reduced by (N, no. of shards), but still exists\n                    => < 100 shards \n                    => still very costly\n                 - Queries latency will slightly increase as has to merge the results from all shards.\n\n        - Main problem with above is, handling locking on disk. \n            - If we can handle the lock contention in memory, that would be a better solution than sharding\n            - Options:\n                - Kafka Streams: Let the view consumer aggregate the results with windowing(can be tumbling or sliding window),\n                  and only push the results to db only every once a sec.\n                    - Throughput of kafka = 100k/s per partition\n                        - But due to aggregations & lock-contentions , this can also come down by a huge number\n                        - partition by videoId:\n                            - Not sure if a single partition can handle a celeb, but better than a single shard.\n                        - partition by (videoId+randomness):\n                            - Best\n                            - Here also kafka has to be horizontally scaled, but to very less number compared to shards.\n                - KStreams VS sharding:\n                    - Adv:\n                        - Less cost:\n                            - No sharding, single instance of postgres. Scaling kafka wont be very costly.\n                            - Both has to be partionioned/sharded by (videoId+randomness), \n                                but the no of kafka consumers needed << no of shards needed.\n                            - This due to:\n                                - In-memory lock contention handling\n                                - Kafka has high throughput than postgres\n                        - Queries faster as No extra merging layer needed, as single postgres instance.\n                    - DisAdv:\n                        - Data loss for unflushed aggregates, as only one consumer consumes from a particular partition.\n                - Strategies to address data loss from consumer:\n                    - Kafka has the data, so reconsume from where data is lost.\n                    - Can use checkpointing to avoid consuming more data during failure.\n                        - every few ms, store the aggregates to s3 \n                    - Flink does this all this by default. So use Flink over KStreams. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
